\begin{document}

\maketitle
\vspace{-1cm}
\begin{abstract}
\noindent 
\footnotesize
Linear estimation of medium-scale DSGE models may compensate for missing nonlinear propagation through inflated shock estimates. I show that the dominant source of the gap between nonlinear and linearized transition functions is the investment channel---investment adjustment costs and capital utilization costs---not the price Phillips curve. In an 18-parameter Smets--Wouters model, a decomposition of 22,080 nonlinear versus linear transition pairs reveals that the investment and capital block accounts for 69 percent of the total nonlinearity gap, while the price Phillips curve accounts for 0.2 percent. I attribute this to quadratic investment adjustment costs and exponential capital utilization costs create state-dependent amplification that first-order perturbation misses entirely. Impulse response comparisons between the full nonlinear solution and first-order perturbation are consistent with this finding: the nonlinear amplification ratio grows with shock magnitude for investment-specific and risk premium shocks, and positive and negative shocks of equal size produce asymmetric responses---a direct signature of the convex cost functions that linear models discard. To make nonlinear estimation feasible, I develop an adaptive multi-fidelity estimator that combines stochastic extended path (SEP) solution with a parameter-conditional neural network surrogate and regime-switching likelihood evaluation. The surrogate learns the nonlinear \textit{correction} to first-order perturbation---not the full model---concentrating capacity on the approximation gap that matters for inference. The regime-switching gate allocates the linear model to tranquil periods and the nonlinear surrogate to stress periods, using state displacement as the trigger since it explains 96 percent of the approximation gap. On US data (1959Q1--2004Q4), preliminary 18-parameter estimation suggests the nonlinear model shifts several posteriors substantially: risk premium shock volatility more than doubles, wage markup persistence drops from 0.37 to 0.08, and Kimball price curvature nearly triples---consistent with capturing investment dynamics through endogenous amplification rather than outsized exogenous shocks, though surrogate approximation error prevents definitive attribution. A synthetic validation exercise recovers three shock standard deviations within 5 percent of truth ($\hat{R} < 1.01$). Offline training costs 12 hours; the full 18-parameter estimation with 50,000 Metropolis--Hastings draws completes in 50 minutes.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent \textbf{JEL Classification:} C11, C13, C32, C63, E47

\noindent \textbf{Keywords:} DSGE models, nonlinear estimation, neural networks, Bayesian inference, occasionally binding constraints, stochastic extended path

