% ============================================================================
% SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION
% ============================================================================

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Estimated DSGE models rely on first-order perturbation for speed, but that approximation discards all curvature. Where does the discarded curvature matter most? The standard conjecture points to the price Phillips curve and the zero lower bound. I find something different. In an 18-parameter Smets--Wouters model, 69 percent of the gap between the nonlinear and linearized transition functions concentrates in the investment and capital block. The price Phillips curve contributes 0.2 percent. A single observable---investment growth---accounts for nearly all of the difference.

The economic mechanism is specific: quadratic investment adjustment costs and exponential capital utilization costs create state-dependent amplification that first-order perturbation drops by construction. A linear model cannot represent the interaction between Tobin's $q$, the adjustment cost derivative $S'$, and the forward-looking Euler wedge, so the linear model may compensate by inflating estimated shock magnitudes.

But nonlinear estimation remains prohibitively slow. A single stochastic extended path (SEP) solve takes 0.8 seconds; a bootstrap particle filter with 100 particles and 184 periods needs roughly one hour per likelihood evaluation. The question is how to allocate nonlinear accuracy where it matters.

I develop an adaptive multi-fidelity estimator with three accuracy levels: first-order perturbation (ROM1) as the cheap baseline, a neural network surrogate trained on SEP solutions as the intermediate layer, and direct SEP as the full-order reference. The surrogate learns the nonlinear \textit{correction} $g^{\text{SEP}} - g^{\text{ROM1}}$, not the full transition---a residual target two orders of magnitude smaller than the original, concentrated in the investment block and smooth in the state-shock space. A regime-switching gate allocates ROM1 to tranquil periods and the nonlinear surrogate to stress periods, triggering on filtered state displacement since it explains 96 percent of the approximation gap. The heavy cost of global nonlinear solution is paid once, offline; the full 18-parameter estimation with 50,000 draws completes in 50 minutes on a single workstation.

In a 3-parameter validation with a binding ZLB, the method recovers shock standard deviations within 5 percent of truth ($\hat{R} < 1.01$). Offline training costs 12 hours; a bootstrap particle filter with exact SEP transitions would need roughly 87 days on a 100-core cluster (Table~\ref{tab:benchmarks}).

On US data (1959Q1--2004Q4), preliminary 18-parameter estimation shows the nonlinear model shifts several posteriors: risk premium shock volatility more than doubles, wage markup persistence drops from 0.37 to 0.08, and Kimball price curvature nearly triples---consistent with the linear model attributing nonlinear investment dynamics to outsized exogenous shocks.

The paper links three literatures. SEP work \citep{fair1983solution,adjemian2025stochastic} provides the nonlinear oracle. Surrogate approximation \citep{koop2022bayesian,maliar2021deep,azinovic2022deep} motivates the acceleration step, though I approximate the transition map rather than the likelihood. Filter-free inference \citep{andrieu2010particle,childers2022filter,farkas2020bayesian} motivates treating shocks as explicit unknowns and sampling with HMC.

Section~\ref{sec:literature} reviews related work. Section~\ref{sec:model} presents the model. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} develops the methodology. Section~\ref{sec:identification} discusses identification and approximation error. Section~\ref{sec:validation_design} describes validation design. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports results. Section~\ref{sec:robustness} reports robustness checks, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.


% ============================================================================
% SECTION 2: RELATED LITERATURE
% ============================================================================

\section{Related Literature}
\label{sec:literature}

% \subsection{Local and Global Solution Methods}

Local perturbation methods \citep{sims2002solving,schmitt2004solving,andreasen2018pruning} remain the workhorse in estimated DSGE models. The limitation is structural: a Taylor expansion around steady state cannot represent kinks from occasionally binding constraints, and approximation error grows with displacement.

Global methods---projection \citep{judd1992projection}, sparse grids \citep{krueger2004computing,malin2011solving}, time iteration \citep{coleman1990solving}---solve the nonlinear system directly. For occasionally binding constraints specifically, piecewise-linear methods such as \citet{guerrieri2015occbin} offer a tractable toolkit that switches between regimes, while \citet{holden2016computation} provides a complementary approach. SEP \citep{fair1983solution,denhaan1990solving,adjemian2013stochastic,adjemian2025stochastic} builds a stochastic tree, approximates expectations with Gauss--Hermite quadrature, and solves the resulting system with Newton steps. SEP handles occasionally binding constraints in medium-scale models and treats the initial state $s_0$ as a boundary condition, supporting arbitrary initial conditions. The barrier is runtime: direct global solves are too slow for repeated likelihood evaluation.

% \subsection{Nonlinear Estimation and Surrogate Acceleration}

Nonlinear estimation is feasible but expensive. \citet{gust2012effects} use regime-specific approximations with particle filtering and Metropolis--Hastings; \citet{boehl2022monetary} estimates a nonlinear model with occasionally binding constraints using piecewise-linear methods; \citet{fernandezvillaverde2021estimating} handle stochastic volatility in dynamic equilibrium models. Surveys such as \citet{fernandez2016solution} and sampling strategies such as \citet{herbst2016sequential} improve stability but still require many expensive model evaluations.

Particle filtering also faces weight degeneracy: during crisis episodes the effective sample size collapses to near unity \citep{gordon1993novel, herbst2019tempered, cubaborda2019likelihood}. Tempering partially helps but raises per-iteration cost 5--10$\times$ and introduces bias. The filter-free approach adopted here sidesteps degeneracy entirely by treating shocks as explicit unknowns.

Surrogate methods pay most cost offline. The multi-fidelity idea---combining cheap and expensive evaluations---has a long history in engineering \citep{peherstorfer2018survey}. Neural surrogates \citep{maliar2021deep,azinovic2022deep,scheidegger2019machine} scale well in high dimensions; \citet{winberry2018method} develops a related approach for solving and estimating heterogeneous agent models, approximating policy functions with neural networks to handle the curse of dimensionality. Two recent papers are especially close. \citet{koop2022bayesian} approximate the likelihood map $\theta \mapsto p(y_{1:T} \mid \theta)$---fast but dataset-specific and without state-level diagnostics. \citet{naubert2025differentiable} approximate the transition function with mixture density networks, but without separating linear and nonlinear components. I combine elements of both with three additions: SEP as the structural teacher, a residual formulation $g^{\text{SEP}} - g^{\text{ROM1}}$, and a regime-switching gate.

% \subsection{Filter-Free Inference and Positioning}

Filter-free inference samples parameters and shocks jointly, avoiding repeated particle filtering. \citet{andrieu2010particle} develop particle MCMC; \citet{childers2022filter} show efficient filter-free HMC in linear settings; \citet{farkas2020bayesian} adapt HMC to standard DSGE estimation and demonstrate its advantages over random walk Metropolis--Hastings, including multimodality detection. This paper extends filter-free HMC to nonlinear DSGE estimation with a differentiable surrogate transition map, combining SEP, transition-map surrogates, and gradient-based sampling in one estimator.

% ============================================================================
% SECTION 3: MODEL ENVIRONMENT
% ============================================================================

\section{Model Environment and Nonlinear Estimation Problem}
\label{sec:model}

This section states the model, defines the estimation target, and describes the New Keynesian environment used in validation.

\subsection{General DSGE Framework}

I start from a DSGE model with nonlinear rational expectations equations:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:equilibrium}
\E_t\left[F(s_{t+1}, s_t, s_{t-1}, \eps_t; \theta)\right] = 0,
\end{equation}
The vector $s_t \in \R^{n_s}$ collects endogenous states and jumps. The shock vector $\eps_t \in \R^{n_\eps}$ follows $\eps_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_{n_\eps})$ after scaling by shock standard deviations inside the model equations. The parameter vector is $\theta \in \Theta \subset \R^{n_\theta}$. The function $F: \R^{n_s} \times \R^{n_s} \times \R^{n_s} \times \R^{n_\eps} \times \Theta \to \R^{n_s}$ collects equilibrium conditions.

A \textit{solution} is a policy function $g_\theta: \R^{n_s} \times \R^{n_\eps} \to \R^{n_s}$ such that
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:policy}
s_t = g_\theta(s_{t-1}, \eps_t),
\end{equation}
and the implied state sequence $\{s_t\}_{t=0}^\infty$ satisfies \eqref{eq:equilibrium} for all $t \geq 0$.

\paragraph{Key departure from local methods.} I do \textit{not} linearize $F$ around steady state. I solve \eqref{eq:equilibrium} on the nonlinear state space, so the model keeps kinks from occasionally binding constraints and other state dependence.

\subsection{Observation Equation}

I map model states to observed data with
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:observation}
y_t = H(s_t) + \eta_t, \quad \eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigmay),
\end{equation}
where $y_t \in \R^{n_y}$ is the vector of observables (typically $n_y \ll n_s$), $H: \R^{n_s} \to \R^{n_y}$ is a (possibly nonlinear) measurement function, and $\eta_t$ represents measurement error.

In the 3-parameter Gal\'i model, $H$ selects output, inflation, and the nominal interest rate ($n_y = 3$). In the 18-parameter Smets--Wouters model, $H$ maps states to seven observables: output growth, consumption growth, investment growth, hours worked, inflation, wage growth, and the interest rate ($n_y = 7$). I calibrate $\Sigmay$ to match data-revision or aggregation noise.

\subsection{Estimation Problem}

Given observed data $\{y_t^{\text{obs}}\}_{t=1}^T$, the estimation target is the posterior distribution of structural parameters:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:posterior}
p(\theta \given y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}}) \propto p(y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}} \given \theta) \, p(\theta),
\end{equation}
where $p(y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}} \given \theta)$ is the likelihood and $p(\theta)$ is the prior.

% \paragraph{Standard approach (linearized).}
 Standard DSGE estimation linearizes \eqref{eq:equilibrium} around steady state, evaluates $p(y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}} \given \theta)$ with a Kalman filter, and samples \eqref{eq:posterior} with random-walk Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC).

% \paragraph{Our approach (nonlinear).} 
This paper keeps the nonlinear structure at every stage. I solve \eqref{eq:equilibrium} globally with SEP on a design set $\{\theta^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N_\theta}$ and train a neural surrogate $\hat{g}(s_{t-1}, \eps_t, \theta; w)$ for the transition map $g_\theta(s_{t-1}, \eps_t)$. I then form the filter-free posterior $p(\theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T} \given y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}})$ and sample it with MCMC using the surrogate's differentiable structure.

The likelihood function in the filter-free approach is constructed as:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:likelihood}
\mathcal{L}(\theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T} \given y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}}) = \prod_{t=1}^T p(y_t^{\text{obs}} \given s_t; \Sigmay) \cdot p(s_0) \cdot \prod_{t=1}^T p(\eps_t),
\end{equation}
where $s_t = \hat{g}(s_{t-1}, \eps_t, \theta; w)$ is the surrogate-predicted state, $p(y_t^{\text{obs}} \given s_t; \Sigmay)$ is the measurement density from \eqref{eq:observation}, $p(s_0)$ is the prior on the initial state, and $p(\eps_t) = \mathcal{N}(0, I_{n_\eps})$ is the shock prior. This formulation treats shocks $\eps_{1:T}$ and initial state $s_0$ as explicit parameters to be sampled jointly with $\theta$, avoiding the need for particle filtering.

\subsection{Illustrative Models}
\label{subsec:gali_model}

The 3-parameter validation uses a New Keynesian model adapted from \citet{gali2015monetary} with Calvo pricing, a Taylor rule, and an occasionally binding ZLB (23 variables, 3 shocks). The Taylor rule is $R_t = \max\{ 1, \, (R_{ss})^{1-\rho_R} (\Pi_t/\Pi_{ss})^{(1-\rho_R)\phi_\pi} (Y_t/Y_{ss})^{(1-\rho_R)\phi_y} R_{t-1}^{\rho_R} e^{\varepsilon_t^R} \}$, where the $\max\{1,\cdot\}$ operator enforces the ZLB. I estimate $\theta = (\sigma_A, \sigma_\mu, \sigma_R)$ holding other parameters at standard calibration ($\beta = 0.99$, $\xi_p = 0.75$, $\phi_\pi = 1.5$, $\phi_y = 0.125$, $\rho_R = 0.8$). Full equations are in Appendix~\ref{app:model}.

The 18-parameter analysis uses a Smets and Wouters (2007) model with habit formation, capital accumulation, investment adjustment costs, variable capital utilization, Kimball demand aggregation, and the ZLB---66 variables (26 states), 7 shocks, and 18 estimated parameters covering shock processes and the price/wage setting block $(\xi_p, \iota_p, \varepsilon_p, \xi_w)$. See Appendix~\ref{app:sw07_model} for the full specification.


% ============================================================================
% SECTION 4: METHODOLOGY
% ============================================================================

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

This section presents the estimator in five parts: the multi-fidelity architecture that organizes the three accuracy levels, SEP solution, surrogate training, regime-switching order of solution, and filter-free HMC.

\subsection{Multi-Fidelity Architecture}
\label{subsec:multifidelity}

The estimator operates on three fidelity levels. Level~0 is first-order perturbation (ROM1): it evaluates in microseconds, provides exact gradients, and serves as the cheap baseline for every period. Level~1 is the neural network surrogate trained on SEP transitions: it evaluates in milliseconds, provides approximate gradients through automatic differentiation, and captures the nonlinear correction to ROM1. Level~2 is direct stochastic extended path (SEP): it evaluates in roughly 0.8~seconds, provides the gold-standard nonlinear transition, and serves as the offline oracle against which all lower levels are measured.

The key design principle is that each level is defined \textit{relative to} the one below it. The surrogate at Level~1 does not learn the full nonlinear transition $g_\theta^{\text{SEP}}(s,\varepsilon)$; it learns the residual $\Delta g = g_\theta^{\text{SEP}} - g_\theta^{\text{ROM1}}$. At inference, the Level~1 prediction is $\hat{g}(s,\varepsilon,\theta) = g_\theta^{\text{ROM1}}(s,\varepsilon) + \hat{\Delta}(s,\varepsilon,\theta; w)$. This residual formulation has three advantages: it reduces the dynamic range of the learning target by two orders of magnitude, it makes the surrogate's error directly interpretable as a deviation from the nonlinear correction, and it guarantees that when the surrogate predicts zero correction the estimator defaults gracefully to ROM1.

The regime-switching gate (Section~\ref{subsec:regime_switching}) allocates fidelity levels to time periods. In the current implementation, the gate switches between Level~0 and Level~1. In principle, the same framework accommodates a three-level gate: Level~0 in tranquil periods, Level~1 in moderate-stress periods, and Level~2 for a small number of critical periods where the surrogate's uncertainty exceeds tolerance. The budget constraint on Level~2 evaluations makes this a constrained optimization problem: given a computational budget of $B$ SEP evaluations per MCMC draw, which $B$ periods should be evaluated at full order? I discuss the connection to oracle regret in Section~\ref{subsec:regime_switching} and leave the three-level implementation for future work.

The architecture solves the \textit{allocation} problem---which periods need nonlinear accuracy---not the approximation problem of making the surrogate arbitrarily accurate. The framework's value proposition is that the distance from the full-order posterior is minimized for a given computational budget.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:estimation} summarizes the complete estimation procedure, showing how the offline and online stages connect.

\begin{algorithmbox}[Multi-Fidelity Estimation]
\label{alg:estimation}
\textbf{Offline stage} (run once):\par
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=2pt]
\item Draw parameter grid $\{\theta^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N_\theta}$ from prior support.
\item For each $\theta^{(i)}$: simulate $N_{\text{sim}}$ periods via SEP (Level~2), producing transitions $(s_{t-1}, \varepsilon_t) \mapsto s_t$.
\item Compute ROM1 transitions at the same inputs; store residuals $\Delta_j = s_t^{\text{SEP}} - s_t^{\text{ROM1}}$.
\item Train neural surrogate $\hat{\Delta}(s,\varepsilon,\theta; w)$ to predict residuals. Validate on held-out test set (RRMSE $< 0.002$).
\item Calibrate gate threshold $\tau$ by comparing ROM1 and surrogate log-likelihoods on holdout data.
\end{enumerate}\par\smallskip
\textbf{Online stage} (per MCMC draw at proposed $\theta'$):\par
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=2pt]
\item Recover shocks: run inversion filter with ROM1 transition to obtain $\hat{\varepsilon}_{1:T}$.
\item For $t=1,\ldots,T$: compute state displacement $d_t = \|s_t - \bar{s}(\theta')\|$.
\item Set gate: $z_t = \mathbb{1}(d_t > \tau)$.
\item Evaluate period likelihood: if $z_t=0$, use Kalman filter (Level~0); if $z_t=1$, use surrogate correction $\hat{g} = g^{\text{ROM1}} + \hat{\Delta}$ (Level~1).
\item Sum log-likelihood: $\ell(\theta') = \sum_t [(1-z_t)\ell_t^{\text{ROM}} + z_t \ell_t^{\text{NL}}]$.
\item Accept/reject $\theta'$ via Metropolis--Hastings ratio.
\end{enumerate}
\end{algorithmbox}

The multi-fidelity design admits a clean error decomposition. Since the observation likelihood factorizes conditionally on the state trajectory, the KL divergence between the mixed posterior and the full-order posterior satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:multifidelity_bound}
D_{\text{KL}}\!\left(\hat{p}_{\text{mixed}} \,\|\, p_2\right) \leq \sum_{t=1}^T \left[(1-z_t)\,\delta_t^{(0)} + z_t\,\delta_t^{(1)}\right],
\end{equation}
where $\delta_t^{(0)}$ and $\delta_t^{(1)}$ are the per-period KL contributions from using ROM1 and the surrogate respectively instead of SEP. The gate's job is to set $z_t = 1$ when the ROM1 error exceeds the surrogate error. Since the nonlinearity decomposition shows that $\delta_t^{(0)}$ is nearly monotonic in state displacement (Section~\ref{subsec:nonlinearity_sources}), a state-distance threshold is a natural implementation.

\subsection{Stochastic Extended Path (SEP) Algorithm}
\label{subsec:sep}

SEP solves the nonlinear model on a stochastic tree and integrates expectations by quadrature. I first state the core logic, then discuss cost and initial conditions.

%\subsubsection{Why Global Solutions Matter}

The DSGE equilibrium is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:equilibrium_system}
\E_t[F(s_{t+1}, s_t, s_{t-1}, \eps_t; \theta)] = 0,
\end{equation}
where $s_t \in \R^{n_s}$ is the vector of endogenous state variables, $\eps_t \in \R^{n_\eps}$ is the vector of exogenous shocks, $\theta \in \R^{n_\theta}$ contains structural parameters, and $F: \R^{3n_s + n_\eps + n_\theta} \to \R^{n_s}$ stacks the first-order conditions, market clearing conditions, and policy rules.

Most estimated DSGE models use perturbation around steady state. That approach is fast, but it can miss regime switches, especially at the ZLB.

The problem is practical, not cosmetic. Large shocks move the economy away from the expansion point, and local terms then give biased dynamics. State dependence also matters: propagation differs across tranquil and crisis states.

SEP avoids this loss by solving \eqref{eq:equilibrium_system} directly at each state-shock input. The solver keeps kinks, asymmetries, and state dependence that local expansions smooth away.

%\subsubsection{The SEP Algorithm: Switching the Order of Approximation}

SEP changes the approximation order. Perturbation approximates the policy function first and then evaluates expectations. SEP approximates expectations first, using quadrature, and then solves the nonlinear system. This order keeps the nonlinear structure of $F$ during solution.

% \paragraph{Step 1: Approximating Expectations via Gauss--Hermite Quadrature.}

I approximate expectations with Gauss--Hermite quadrature on a product grid of $K$ nodes per shock dimension, yielding $K^{n_\eps}$ evaluation points. The choice of $K$ depends on the number of shocks: I use $K=5$ for the 3-parameter validation model ($n_\eps=3$, giving 125 nodes per period) and $K=3$ for the 18-parameter HLT model ($n_\eps=7$, giving $3^7=2{,}187$ nodes per period). The node and weight formulas and convergence properties are standard; see Appendix~\ref{app:sep} for details.

%\paragraph{Step 2: Constructing the Stochastic Path Tree.}

SEP builds a branching tree that fans out for $H_{\text{branch}}$ periods (typically 2--3) and then collapses each branch to a deterministic tail with zero future shocks. This funnel structure resolves near-term uncertainty---where nonlinear effects matter most---while keeping tree size manageable. A terminal condition $s_{T_{\text{SEP}}+1} = \bar{s}(\theta)$ closes the system. With $H_{\text{branch}}=2$ and $K=5$ in the 3-shock validation model, the effective tree has roughly 250 nodes per root; with $K=3$ in the 7-shock HLT model, the tree has approximately 54 nodes per root. The node-count arithmetic is in Appendix~\ref{app:sep}.

%\paragraph{Step 3: Solving the Nonlinear System.}

Stacking equilibrium conditions across all tree nodes yields a residual system $R(Y;\theta)=0$, where $Y$ collects state variables at every node. I solve this system with damped Newton--Raphson:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:newton_iteration}
Y^{(j+1)} = Y^{(j)} - \alpha^{(j)} \left[\frac{\partial R}{\partial Y}(Y^{(j)}; \theta)\right]^{-1} R(Y^{(j)}; \theta),
\end{equation}
where $\alpha^{(j)} \in (0,1]$ is a line-search parameter and the Jacobian is computed via automatic differentiation. Convergence details, including iteration counts and residual tolerances, are in Appendix~\ref{app:sep}.

\paragraph{Convergence.}
The damped Newton method achieves local quadratic convergence when the Jacobian is nonsingular. Binding constraints reduce convergence to superlinear (the Jacobian is discontinuous at the binding boundary). In practice, warm starts require 3--5 iterations in the unconstrained regime and 8--15 when binding status changes. Backtracking line search (Armijo condition) guarantees global convergence to a stationary point of $\|R\|^2$.

%\paragraph{Step 4: Extracting the Implied Transition Function.}

Once the system converges, the solved tree defines a deterministic transition function for each shock realization:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:transition_map}
s_{t+1} = g_\theta(s_t, \eps_t),
\end{equation}
which maps the lagged state and current shock to the next-period state, conditional on parameters $\theta$. It is this function $g_\theta$ that I approximate using a neural network in the next subsection.

%\subsubsection{Computational Cost}

A single SEP solve takes about 0.8 seconds on the reference hardware. The cost is dominated by the Newton iteration in~\eqref{eq:newton_iteration}: each iteration requires assembling and factorizing the Jacobian $\partial R / \partial Y$, which has dimension $(T_{\text{SEP}} \times n_s) \times (T_{\text{SEP}} \times n_s)$. For the 66-variable Smets--Wouters model with $T_{\text{SEP}}=40$, this is a $2{,}640 \times 2{,}640$ sparse system. The Jacobian is block-tridiagonal (each period's equations depend on neighboring periods only), so factorization costs $\mathcal{O}(T_{\text{SEP}} \cdot n_s^3)$ per Newton step. Typical convergence requires 5--20 Newton steps, with warm starts reducing this to 3--5 when consecutive evaluations share similar parameter values.

Embedding SEP directly in MCMC with 10,000 draws and $T=100$ periods would require roughly 9 days; adding particle filtering would push cost to years. The surrogate pays the SEP cost once offline, then evaluates in milliseconds during inference (see Appendix~\ref{app:sep} for the full cost breakdown). The multi-fidelity architecture (Section~\ref{subsec:multifidelity}) exploits the four-order-of-magnitude gap between ROM1 ($\sim10^{-5}$~s), surrogate ($\sim10^{-3}$~s), and SEP ($\sim10^{0}$~s) evaluation costs to allocate accuracy efficiently across time periods.

%\subsubsection{Occasionally Binding Constraints}

SEP naturally handles occasionally binding constraints by incorporating them directly into $F$. For the zero lower bound, I implement:
\begin{equation}
R_t = \max\{1, \, \tilde{R}_t\},
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{R}_t$ is the unconstrained Taylor rule rate from \eqref{eq:taylor_zlb_full}. When $\tilde{R}_t < 1$, the constraint binds and $R_t = 1$.

The Newton solver in \eqref{eq:newton_iteration} handles the kink with a subdifferential update. When the constraint binds, the solver sets the constrained gradient component to zero and iterates on the remaining free variables. Binding episodes add iterations, but they do not change the basic complexity class.

%\subsubsection{Initial Condition Flexibility}
\label{subsubsec:flexible_init}

SEP treats $s_0$ as a boundary condition, not a steady-state requirement---the economy can start from any initial state. This enables warm starts across nearby parameter values (cutting Newton iterations from $\sim$20 to 3--5, a 4--6$\times$ speedup) and lets HMC learn $s_0$ from the data rather than imposing an ergodic initialization.

\paragraph{State-dependent impulse responses.}
A consequence of global solution is that impulse responses become state-dependent. The generalized IRF \citep{koop1996impulse} is $\text{GIRF}_h(s_t, \varepsilon_t; \theta) = \mathbb{E}_t[s_{t+h} \mid s_t, \varepsilon_t] - \mathbb{E}_t[s_{t+h} \mid s_t, \varepsilon_t = 0]$, which I compute as the difference between shocked and unshocked SEP simulations. Under perturbation this collapses to the state-independent linear IRF $\Phi^h B\varepsilon_t$. Under SEP, nonlinear propagation diverges from the linear benchmark as the economy moves away from steady state, producing time-varying amplification even with fixed structural parameters.

%\subsubsection{Expectation Approximation: Gauss--Hermite vs HMC}
\label{subsubsec:hmc_sep}

Gauss--Hermite quadrature is the default expectation approximation. When fixed nodes cause solver failures near constraint kinks, I switch to HMC-based expectation approximation, which is 2--3$\times$ slower but achieves 67--80\% convergence where Gauss--Hermite falls below 10\%. Details are in Appendix~\ref{app:sep}.


\subsection{Dataset Generation and Surrogate Training}
\label{subsec:surrogate}
\label{subsec:surrogate_training}

The SEP transition map is deterministic---the same $(s_{t-1},\eps_t,\theta)$ always produces the same $s_t$---so the problem reduces to supervised learning on labeled transitions. A single network takes $(s_{t-1},\eps_t,\theta)$ as input and predicts $s_t$, conditioning on parameters so the same surrogate serves all posterior draws without retraining. This moves the SEP cost offline and keeps online evaluation in milliseconds.

%\subsubsection{Parameter Grid Design}

I select a design set $\{\theta^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N_\theta}$ that covers prior support. In the three-parameter exercise, which estimates shock standard deviations $(\sigma_A, \sigma_\mu, \sigma_R)$, I use a Sobol sequence because its low-discrepancy property delivers broad coverage with fewer draws than purely random sampling. The baseline grid size is $N_\theta=50$ in the three-parameter case and $N_\theta \approx 120$ in the 18-parameter case, and each parameter draw is restricted to its prior support, such as $\sigma_A \sim \text{Uniform}(0.005,0.02)$. In higher dimensions I augment the Sobol design with $N_{\text{extra}}=50$ prior draws to increase density in high-probability regions and reduce interpolation error near posterior mass.

%\subsubsection{Simulation and Training Data Extraction}

For each design point $\theta^{(i)}$, I simulate $N_{\text{sim}}=200$ periods. The path starts from an initial state $s_0$, which is typically $\bar{s}(\theta^{(i)})$ but can be set away from steady state because SEP treats the initial condition as a boundary condition rather than a fixed point requirement; see Section~\ref{subsubsec:flexible_init}. For each period $t=1,\ldots,N_{\text{sim}}$, I draw $\eps_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I_{n_\eps})$, solve SEP to obtain $s_t = g_{\theta^{(i)}}(s_{t-1}, \eps_t)$, and store the supervised pair
\begin{align*}
x_t &= (s_{t-1}, \eps_t, \theta^{(i)}) \in \R^{n_s + n_\eps + n_\theta}, \\
y_t &= s_t \in \R^{n_s}.
\end{align*}

This yields $N_{\text{train}} = N_\theta \times N_{\text{sim}}$ training pairs. For the 3-parameter case with $N_\theta = 50$ and $N_{\text{sim}} = 200$, this yields $10{,}000$ training samples. For the 18-parameter case with $N_\theta = 120$ parameter configurations and 184 usable periods per configuration, the total is $22{,}080$ training samples.

After discarding initial transients, the usable dataset contains $50 \times 180 = 9{,}000$ samples (3-parameter) or $120 \times 184 = 22{,}080$ samples (18-parameter).

%\subsubsection{Neural Network Architecture}

The surrogate is a feedforward network with two hidden layers (256 and 128 neurons), taking $(s_{t-1}, \eps_t, \theta)$ as input and predicting $s_t$. I use $\tanh$ activation in the 3-parameter case and SiLU in the 18-parameter case, where the latter avoids saturation when the filter visits states far from the training mean. Both inputs and outputs are standardized to zero mean and unit variance. A comparison of five architectures in Appendix~\ref{app:surrogate} (Table~\ref{tab:architecture_comparison}) confirms that the [256, 128] configuration dominates on accuracy per training hour; wider and deeper networks provide negligible accuracy gains.

%\subsubsection{Training Procedure}

\paragraph{ROM1 residual training.}
Rather than training the network to predict full SEP transitions directly, I train it to predict the \textit{residual} between SEP and ROM1:
\begin{equation}
\Delta y_j = g_\theta^{\text{SEP}}(x_j) - g_\theta^{\text{ROM1}}(x_j).
\end{equation}
At inference, the surrogate prediction is $\hat{y} = g^{\text{ROM1}}(x) + \hat{\Delta}(x; w)$. This residual formulation reduces the dynamic range of the training target (the residual is typically two orders of magnitude smaller than the full transition) and concentrates network capacity on the nonlinear correction that matters. In the 18-parameter case, the surrogate achieves 96--99 percent improvement over ROM1 per output dimension.

The residual formulation acts as a \textit{control variate}: ROM1 is computable in closed form, highly correlated with $g^{\text{SEP}}$ ($\rho > 0.99$ near steady state, $> 0.95$ in the most displaced quintile), and subtracting it reduces effective training-target variance by 50--100$\times$.

I minimize mean squared error (MSE) over normalized residual outputs:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}(w) = \frac{1}{N_{\text{train}}} \sum_{j=1}^{N_{\text{train}}} \|\hat{\Delta}(\tilde{x}_j; w) - \widetilde{\Delta y}_j\|^2,
\end{equation}
where $w$ denotes the network weights.

%\paragraph{Optimizer.} 
I use Adam \citep{kingma2014adam} with learning rate $\eta=0.001$, decay parameters $(\beta_1,\beta_2)=(0.9,0.999)$, and mini-batches of size 64. A grid search over 12 architecture--activation--learning-rate combinations (Table~\ref{tab:hyperparameter_grid}) confirms this configuration.

% \paragraph{Regularization.} 
I split the dataset into 80 percent training and 20 percent validation samples, monitor validation loss every ten epochs, stop training when validation loss fails to improve for twenty checkpoints, and restore the best checkpoint. I do not add explicit L2 penalties because early stopping is sufficient at these sample sizes.

% \paragraph{Training duration.} 
Typically 200--500 epochs to convergence (approximately 30--60 minutes on a single CPU core, or 5--10 minutes on a GPU). For the 18-parameter case with 22,080 samples, training takes roughly 2 hours on CPU.

%\subsubsection{Validation and Error Metrics}

I assess surrogate quality on a held-out test set of 2,000 samples from 10 parameter draws excluded from training, using relative RMSE (RRMSE $=$ RMSE$/\text{std}(y)$) as the primary metric. The acceptance threshold is RRMSE $< 0.002$, i.e., approximation error below 0.2 percent of output standard deviation. In practice the 256--128 architecture achieves RRMSE $\approx 0.0008$ for the 3-parameter case and $\approx 0.0012$ for the 18-parameter case.

\paragraph{Approximation guarantees.}
The residual target $\Delta g = g^{\text{SEP}} - g^{\text{ROM1}}$ is smooth on the state-space interior and small in magnitude (two orders below the full transition), so moderate-sized networks (49,562 parameters) suffice despite 51-dimensional input. This favorable scaling is a direct consequence of the residual formulation.

\paragraph{Surrogate uncertainty.}
The point-estimate surrogate carries no epistemic uncertainty. Adding calibrated uncertainty---via deep ensembles, a Bayesian last layer, or conformal prediction bands---would enable uncertainty-triggered fallback to direct SEP and likelihood inflation in high-uncertainty periods. The current implementation handles this indirectly through RRMSE monitoring and distance-quintile diagnostics (Section~\ref{sec:identification}).

\paragraph{Training distribution anchoring.}
Unconditional simulations from steady state can produce states far from the inference region (z-scores of 3--6 in the 18-parameter case). I augment with ROM1-filtered state trajectories from observed data: roughly half the training set comes from standard simulations (broad coverage), half from ROM1-anchored paths (inference coverage), reducing the maximum z-score from 6.2 to $\approx$1.5.


\subsection{Regime-Switching Order-of-Solution Estimator}
\label{subsec:regime_switching}

The estimator switches the order of solution over time. In periods close to linear dynamics, it uses a cheap ROM (first-order perturbation). In periods where constraints bind or stress metrics are high, it uses the nonlinear surrogate. Let $z_t = \mathbb{1}(m_t > \tau)$ denote the regime indicator, where $m_t$ is a stress metric (e.g., the shadow-rate gap or a filtered shock magnitude) and $\tau$ is a calibrated threshold. Define two transition maps:
\begin{align}
s_t^{\text{ROM}} &= g_\theta^{\text{ROM}}(s_{t-1}, \eps_t), \\
s_t^{\text{NL}}  &= \hat{g}_\theta(s_{t-1}, \eps_t),
\end{align}
where $g_\theta^{\text{ROM}}$ is the low-order reduced map and $\hat{g}_\theta$ is the nonlinear surrogate trained on SEP data. The switched transition is
\begin{equation}
s_t = (1-z_t)\,s_t^{\text{ROM}} + z_t\,s_t^{\text{NL}}.
\end{equation}

This gives a period-by-period likelihood decomposition:
\begin{equation}
\log p(y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}}\mid\theta,s_0,\eps_{1:T},z_{1:T})
= \sum_{t=1}^T \left[(1-z_t)\,\ell_t^{\text{ROM}} + z_t\,\ell_t^{\text{NL}}\right].
\end{equation}
So the estimator is transparent. I report how much fit comes from the ROM regime and how much fit comes from the nonlinear regime.

For benchmarking, direct SEP serves as the full-order model (FOM) reference. On a fixed $\theta$ panel, I compare switched likelihood values against direct SEP likelihood values and report discrepancies and runtime.

\paragraph{Gate design and oracle regret.}
The gate uses state displacement $\|s_t - \bar{s}(\theta)\|$ as the stress metric, justified by the decomposition in Section~\ref{subsec:nonlinearity_sources}: state distance explains 96 percent of the FOM--ROM1 gap. Define the per-period likelihood gain $\Delta\ell_t = \ell_t^{\text{NL}} - \ell_t^{\text{ROM}}$. An oracle with budget $B$ selects the $B$ periods with the largest gains; the oracle regret of any gate $z_{1:T}$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:oracle_regret}
\mathcal{R}_B(z; \theta) = \sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}^*_B} \Delta\ell_t - \sum_{t=1}^T z_t\,\Delta\ell_t \geq 0.
\end{equation}
Since the correlation between $\|s_t - \bar{s}\|$ and $\Delta\ell_t$ is 0.96, the state-distance trigger closely approximates the oracle ranking. Formally minimizing oracle regret through a learned gate, or extending to three levels (ROM1/surrogate/SEP), is left for future work.

\paragraph{Inversion filter for shock recovery.}
For the 18-parameter estimation, I use a per-period inversion filter rather than sampling shocks explicitly. Given state $s_{t-1}$ and observation $y_t^{\text{obs}}$, solve for $\hat{\eps}_t = \argmin_{\eps} \| (y_t^{\text{obs}} - Hg^{\text{ROM1}}(s_{t-1}, \eps, \theta))/\sigma_y \|^2 + \| \eps/\sigma_\eps \|^2$. This reduces the sampling dimension to $n_\theta$ at the cost of conditioning on ROM1-recovered shocks. The conditioning creates a subtle inconsistency: shocks are recovered under the linear map, but the likelihood for gated periods uses the nonlinear surrogate. The gap is small when the surrogate correction is small relative to the ROM1 transition, but iterated inversion (recovering shocks under the surrogate, re-evaluating, and repeating) would be fully consistent; I leave this for future work.

\subsection{Filter-Free Hamiltonian Monte Carlo}
\label{subsec:hmc}

Rather than integrating out latent states, I treat shocks $\eps_{1:T}$ and initial state $s_0$ as explicit unknowns and sample from the augmented posterior:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:augmented_posterior}
p(\theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T} \given y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}}) \propto p(y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}} \given \theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T}) \, p(\eps_{1:T}) \, p(s_0 \given \theta) \, p(\theta).
\end{equation}
The sampling dimension rises from $n_\theta$ to $n_\theta + n_s + Tn_\eps$ (625 in the baseline case), but HMC scales efficiently when gradients are available.

%\subsubsection{Likelihood Construction}

Given $(\theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T})$, I simulate forward using the surrogate, $s_t = \hat{g}(s_{t-1}, \eps_t, \theta; w^*)$, and map states to observables via $y_t^{\text{obs}} = Hs_t + \eta_t$ with $\eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigmay)$. The log-likelihood is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:loglik}
\log p(y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}} \given \theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T}) = -\frac{1}{2} \sum_{t=1}^T \left[ (y_t^{\text{obs}} - H s_t)^\top \Sigmay^{-1} (y_t^{\text{obs}} - H s_t) + \log |\Sigmay| \right],
\end{equation}
dropping the constant $-\frac{n_y}{2}\log(2\pi)$ per period, which cancels in posterior ratios.

%\subsubsection{Prior Specifications}

The joint prior factorizes as $p(\theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T}) = p(\theta)\,p(s_0\given\theta)\prod_t p(\eps_t)$, where structural parameters follow Gamma or Beta priors (detailed in Section~\ref{sec:results}), the initial state is centered at the model steady state $s_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(\bar{s}(\theta), \Sigma_{s_0})$, and innovations are i.i.d.\ standard normal.

%\subsubsection{Hamiltonian Monte Carlo Mechanics}

I sample from the augmented posterior using the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) \citep{hoffman2014nuts}, which adaptively selects trajectory lengths via leapfrog integration. The key computational advantage is that likelihood gradients are computed by automatic differentiation through the surrogate forward simulation---no adjoint model or finite differences are needed. A single HMC iteration takes about 600 ms ($T=200$), so 2,000 posterior draws complete in roughly 45 minutes including NUTS warmup and adaptation. I assess convergence using the Gelman--Rubin statistic ($\hat{R}<1.01$) across four independent chains, with effective sample sizes exceeding 400 per parameter. The Hamiltonian setup, leapfrog equations, and diagnostic formulas are in Appendix~\ref{app:hmc}.


\section{Identification and Approximation Error Decomposition}
\label{sec:identification}

The estimator has three approximation layers: SEP discretization, surrogate approximation, and MCMC sampling error. This section separates those layers and explains where nonlinear dynamics add identification power.


\subsection{Sources of Uncertainty}

Uncertainty in $p(\theta, s_0, \eps_{1:T} \mid y_{1:T}^{\text{obs}})$ comes from structural parameters, latent shocks, and the initial state. Two additional sources sit outside the posterior formula but matter in practice: model misspecification and surrogate approximation error. I handle misspecification through robustness checks and handle surrogate error through held-out diagnostics. Posterior uncertainty then reflects data information subject to nonlinear model restrictions.


\subsection{Nonlinear Identification}

Nonlinear dynamics add identifying variation that linearized models discard. At the ZLB, the kinked policy rule $R_t = \max\{1, \tilde{R}_t\}$ changes the mapping between observables and parameters when the constraint binds. Nonlinear impulse responses are asymmetric---a large negative demand shock triggers different dynamics than the corresponding positive shock. Higher moments (skewness, kurtosis) in observed series become informative about parameters governing regime frequency and persistence.

These channels suggest that the nonlinear Fisher information matrix may satisfy $\mathcal{I}_T^{\text{NL}}(\theta) \succeq \mathcal{I}_T^{\text{lin}}(\theta)$, with strict inequality when the constraint binds. The heuristic argument follows the data processing inequality: the linear state $s_t^{\text{lin}}$ is a projection of $s_t^{\text{NL}}$ onto the tangent space at steady state, and projection can only reduce information. A formal proof requires regularity conditions at the constraint kink that are non-trivial to verify. However, the empirical evidence is mixed: in the 18-parameter estimation, the risk premium shock $\sigma_b$ widens substantially under the nonlinear model (90\% CI from $[0.09, 0.17]$ to $[0.11, 1.06]$), which could reflect a richer parametric landscape but also surrogate approximation error, differential MCMC convergence, or a genuine loss of precision for some parameters. The information ordering likely holds parameter-by-parameter in some dimensions and fails in others, depending on where nonlinear dynamics add curvature to the likelihood surface.


\subsection{Approximation Error Decomposition}

Three approximation layers separate the estimated posterior from the truth. SEP discretization error is $\mathcal{O}(K^{-r/n_\eps}) + \mathcal{O}(e^{-\lambda T_{\text{sim}}})$, negligible at $K=3$. Surrogate error (validation RRMSE $< 0.001$) is two orders of magnitude smaller than typical shock realizations. MCMC error is controlled by standard $\hat{R}$/ESS diagnostics. The total KL divergence decomposes additively:
\begin{equation}
\text{KL}\left(\hat{p}(\theta \mid y_{1:T}) \,\|\, p(\theta \mid y_{1:T})\right) \leq \text{KL}_{\text{SEP}} + \text{KL}_{\text{surr}} + \text{KL}_{\text{MCMC}},
\end{equation}
with the surrogate term dominating in practice. Parameter recovery errors stay below 5 percent in the 3-parameter validation.

The log-likelihood bias from surrogate error is bounded by
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ll_bias_bound}
|\log \hat{p}(y_{1:T} \mid \theta) - \log p(y_{1:T} \mid \theta)| \leq \frac{T}{2\sigma_y^2} \|H\|^2 \, \text{RMSE}_{\text{surr}}^2 + \mathcal{O}(\text{RMSE}^3).
\end{equation}
For the 18-parameter case ($\text{RMSE}_{\text{surr}} \approx 1.2$, $T=184$, $\sigma_y \approx 0.5$), this gives $\approx$442 nats---comparable to the 68-point Kalman--RS gap. Surrogate error is not negligible; ROM1-anchored training and larger datasets are the main levers for reduction.

\paragraph{Posterior distortion as the central diagnostic.}
The right metric is \textit{posterior distortion}, not path RMSE. In the 3-parameter case, parameter recovery within 5 percent with correct coverage bounds the distortion indirectly. In the 18-parameter case, the FOM benchmark (feasible on a cluster per Table~\ref{tab:benchmarks}) is needed to cleanly separate nonlinear dynamics from approximation error. Until then, the posterior shifts should be treated as directionally informative but not definitive.


\subsection{Failure-Aware Design}

The design embeds explicit diagnostics at each layer. SEP solver failures (residual norms exceeding 0.35) trigger recomputation; roughly 1 percent of solves are discarded. Surrogate accuracy is tracked at three levels: aggregate RRMSE on a held-out set (threshold: 0.001), RMSE by distance quintile, and per-observable improvement ratios relative to ROM1. The log-likelihood bias bound in~\eqref{eq:ll_bias_bound} provides a diagnostic shortcut: if the implied log-likelihood perturbation is small relative to the posterior curvature, the posterior shift is bounded. In the 3-parameter case, the perturbation is less than 0.1 nats per period; in the 18-parameter case it is larger, motivating the ROM1-anchored training procedure. Robustness checks in Section~\ref{sec:robustness} stress each failure channel deliberately.


\section{Validation Design}
\label{sec:validation_design}

Validation uses synthetic data with known truth $\theta_0$. This section defines data generation, acceptance rules, benchmarks, and the initial-condition stress test.


\subsection{Synthetic Data Generation}

I use two exercises: a 3-parameter block for transparent recovery and the full 18-parameter block for scalability. Both use the same regime-switching estimator from Section~\ref{subsec:regime_switching}. Synthetic data is generated by simulating the SEP model at true parameters $\theta_0$ from deterministic steady state with i.i.d.\ shocks, extracting observables, and adding measurement error ($\sigma_y = [0.005, 0.002, 0.001]$ for output, inflation, and the interest rate). The sample length is $T=200$ quarters.


\subsection{Acceptance Criteria}

Success requires five criteria: (C1) parameter recovery within 5\% relative error; (C2) 90\% credible interval coverage for all parameters; (C3) shock recovery correlation $>0.7$ with truth; (C4) MCMC convergence ($\hat{R} < 1.01$, ESS $> 1000$); and (C5) non-degenerate regime switching with stress periods assigned to the nonlinear branch.


\subsection{Benchmark Configurations}

I compare the surrogate estimator against three benchmarks:

\paragraph{Benchmark 1: Direct SEP/FOM reference.} Replace the nonlinear surrogate branch with direct SEP evaluations on a fixed $\theta$ panel. This gives the FOM reference for the switched estimator and quantifies surrogate approximation error in the nonlinear regime. Runtime is much higher than the switched operational estimator.

\paragraph{Benchmark 2: Kalman filter with linear approximation.} Use first-order perturbation and Kalman filtering. This is the standard approach but ignores nonlinearities. Divergence from the proposed method quantifies the empirical importance of nonlinear effects.

\paragraph{Benchmark 3: Second-order approximation.} Schmitt-Grohé \& Uribe pruning with particle filtering. This captures some nonlinear effects but cannot handle occasionally binding constraints. Where applicable (away from ZLB), comparison isolates the contribution of global solution methods.

\paragraph{Why no bootstrap particle filter benchmark.}
A bootstrap PF with 100 particles over $T=184$ periods costs $\approx$147 seconds per draw on 100 cores, totaling $\approx$85 machine-days for 50,000 draws (Table~\ref{tab:benchmarks}). More fundamentally, the PF is least reliable when nonlinear estimation matters most: during crisis episodes the effective sample size collapses to near unity \citep{herbst2019tempered, cubaborda2019likelihood}. Tempering \citep{herbst2019tempered} partially ameliorates this but multiplies per-iteration cost 5--10$\times$ and introduces bias. The filter-free formulation avoids weight degeneracy entirely by treating shocks as explicit unknowns.


\subsection{Robustness to Initial Conditions}
\label{subsec:init_cond_robust}

A key claim of SEP is initial-condition flexibility. I test that claim by re-estimating the same synthetic dataset under three priors for $s_0$: a steady-state-centered prior, a displaced prior shifted by $2\sigma_s$, and a crisis prior centered on binding-ZLB states.

I run the full pipeline for each prior. If the boundary-condition treatment is implemented correctly, posterior modes should differ only within MCMC error and credible intervals should overlap strongly. Large differences would indicate hidden steady-state restrictions in the solve or inference code.

These robustness checks are not part of the current validation results reported in Section~\ref{sec:results}; implementing them is a priority for the next revision.


\subsection{Computational Workflow}

The pipeline proceeds in two stages. Offline: generate parameter grid, solve SEP at each grid point, train the surrogate, validate on a held-out set (RRMSE $< 0.002$), and calibrate the gate threshold---roughly two hours on baseline hardware. Online: run NUTS for 10,000 iterations across 4 chains and compute diagnostics---roughly fifteen minutes.


\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

This section reports the main empirical findings: the nonlinearity decomposition, state-dependent impulse responses, ROM1 forecast error analysis, synthetic validation, and the full 18-parameter estimation on US data.

\subsection{Where Nonlinearity Matters: Equation-Block Decomposition}
\label{subsec:nonlinearity_sources}

Each training sample records both the FOM (SEP) and ROM1 (first-order perturbation) transitions at the same $(s_{t-1}, \varepsilon_t, \theta)$. The difference isolates the nonlinear correction the surrogate must learn. I define the scalar nonlinearity measure as
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nonlinearity_measure}
\delta_j = \| g_\theta^{\text{FOM}}(s_{j}, \varepsilon_j) - g_\theta^{\text{ROM1}}(s_{j}, \varepsilon_j) \|_2,
\end{equation}
where $g^{\text{FOM}}$ is the SEP solution and $g^{\text{ROM1}}$ is first-order perturbation. The decomposition draws on 22,080 training samples varying all 18 parameters. Figure~\ref{fig:nonlinearity_decomposition} summarizes: state distance dominates ($R^2 = 0.96$), parameters contribute modestly (11\%), and current-period shocks are negligible ($<0.3$\%).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_nonlinearity_combined.pdf}
\caption{Nonlinearity variance decomposition and surrogate accuracy by distance from steady state. Panel~(a): $R^2$ from regressing the nonlinearity measure $\delta_j = \|g^{\text{FOM}} - g^{\text{ROM1}}\|_2$ on each feature set separately. Panel~(b): RMSE of the neural surrogate (blue) and first-order perturbation ROM1 (red) across quintiles of state distance $\|s - \bar{s}(\theta)\|_2$, with percentage improvement labeled. The surrogate's RMSE is roughly flat while ROM1 errors grow by two orders of magnitude. Based on 22,080 training samples from the 18-parameter dataset.}
\label{fig:nonlinearity_decomposition}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{State distance dominates.}

Regressing $\delta_j$ on state distance $d_j = \|s_j - \bar{s}(\theta_j)\|_2$ alone yields $R^2 = 0.960$. First-order perturbation is exact at steady state and its error grows with displacement; the strength of this relationship means the nonlinear correction is almost entirely a function of where the economy sits, not of which parameters generated that displacement. Table~\ref{tab:error_by_distance} confirms: ROM1 errors grow by two orders of magnitude from the nearest to the most distant quintile, while the surrogate's error stays roughly flat, achieving 99.0 percent RMSE improvement in the farthest quintile.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Surrogate and ROM1 Accuracy by Distance from Steady State}
\label{tab:error_by_distance}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Quintile & Distance Range & $N$ & Surrogate RMSE & ROM1 RMSE & Improvement \\
\midrule
Q1 (nearest) & $[26.4, 36.1]$ & 4{,}600 & 1.217 & 2.139 & 43.1\% \\
Q2 & $[36.1, 40.3]$ & 4{,}600 & 1.116 & 2.297 & 51.4\% \\
Q3 & $[40.3, 46.5]$ & 4{,}600 & 1.165 & 3.318 & 64.9\% \\
Q4 & $[46.5, 56.8]$ & 4{,}600 & 1.209 & 4.264 & 71.6\% \\
Q5 (farthest) & $[56.8, 2542]$ & 4{,}600 & 2.082 & 207.7 & 99.0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\smallskip\footnotesize
Notes: Distance is Euclidean norm $\|s - \bar{s}(\theta)\|_2$. RMSE is over all $n_s$ state variables. Improvement is defined as $1 - \text{RMSE}_{\text{surrogate}}/\text{RMSE}_{\text{ROM1}}$. Based on 22,080 training samples from the 18-parameter dataset.
\end{table}


\paragraph{Investment dynamics dominate the nonlinearity gap.}

Which equations in the model generate the largest FOM--ROM1 differences? I decompose the total $L^2$ gap across all output dimensions into six economic blocks: (i)~investment and capital accumulation, (ii)~Euler equation and consumption, (iii)~wage Phillips curve, (iv)~output and production, (v)~price Phillips curve, and (vi)~monetary policy. Table~\ref{tab:equation_block} reports each block's share of the total squared gap, averaged over all 22,080 training samples.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Equation-Block Decomposition of the FOM--ROM1 Gap}
\label{tab:equation_block}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Economic Block & Variables & Mean $\|\Delta\|_2$ & $L^2$ Share (\%) \\
\midrule
Investment / Capital & $\Delta i$, $k$, $q$, $z$, $r^k$, $a(z)$, $S'$, $q^s$ & 20.53 & 68.9 \\
Euler / Consumption & $\xi$, $c$, $\Delta c$ & 9.59 & 13.5 \\
Wage Phillips Curve & $w$, $\Delta w$, $\dot{w}$, $\gamma^w_{1{-}3}$, $s^w$ & 19.41 & 10.8 \\
Output / Production & $y$, $\Delta y$, hours, $S^D$ & 5.10 & 4.3 \\
Flex-Price Block & $c^f$, $y^f$, $i^f$, $k^f$, $q^f$, $r^{k,f}$ & 3.30 & 2.3 \\
Price Phillips Curve & $\pi$, $D_p$, $\dot{p}$, $\gamma_{1{-}3}$, $s^\pi$ & 1.85 & 0.2 \\
Monetary Policy & $r$, $r^{\text{obs}}$, $m$ & 0.61 & 0.1 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize Notes: Based on 22,080 training samples from the 18-parameter dataset.} \\
\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize $L^2$ share is the fraction of total $\sum_j \|\Delta_j\|_2^2$ attributable to each block.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The result is stark: the investment and capital block accounts for 68.9 percent of the total nonlinearity gap, and a single observable---investment growth $\Delta i$---accounts for 68.8 percent by itself. The price Phillips curve, which is where the Kimball aggregator enters and which receives much attention in the nonlinear DSGE literature, contributes just 0.2 percent of the gap. The monetary policy block contributes 0.1 percent---unsurprising given that the ZLB does not bind at the training shock scale, but the investment block dominates regardless.

I attribute this to the interaction of three nonlinear functions in the investment block (Appendix~\ref{app:sw07_model}): the exponential capital utilization cost $a(z_t)$, the quadratic investment adjustment cost $S(I_t/I_{t-1})$, and Tobin's $q$ dynamics. A negative $q^s$ shock depresses the shadow price $p^k$, which raises adjustment costs for future recovery---a nonlinear investment channel absent in the linearized model, which captures the first-order effect of $q^s$ on $p^k$ but misses the compounding $S' \times p^k \times \xi_{t+1}/\xi_t$ interaction. The Gali (2015) model, which lacks investment entirely, shows small FOM--ROM1 gaps even at $15\sigma$, consistent with the investment/capital channel being the dominant nonlinearity source in this model and calibration.

\paragraph{Time-varying dynamics: nonlinearity versus learning.}
The state dependence of the FOM transition is conceptually distinct from the time-varying parameter literature. \citet{slobodyan2012learning} show time-varying impulse responses under constant-gain learning, where variation arises because agents update their forecast coefficients. Here the time variation comes from the nonlinear equilibrium conditions under rational expectations: during an investment boom, elevated Tobin's $q$ and high marginal adjustment cost $S'$ amplify negative shocks; during recessions, the flatter region of $a(z)$ attenuates the response. The distinction matters for policy: under learning, a central bank can stabilize dynamics by anchoring expectations; under nonlinear propagation, the time variation persists because it comes from the shape of cost functions that communication cannot alter.

\paragraph{Structural parameters shape the nonlinear landscape.}

Structural parameters explain 11 percent of nonlinearity variation ($R^2 = 0.112$). Table~\ref{tab:param_nonlinearity} reports univariate correlations. Government spending persistence $\rho_g$ (0.176) and risk premium volatility $\sigma_b$ (0.169) correlate positively---persistent or volatile shocks push the economy farther from steady state. The Kimball curvature $\varepsilon_p$ correlates \textit{negatively} ($-0.108$): higher curvature flattens demand, compresses price dispersion, and makes equilibrium dynamics more linear despite steeper equation-level nonlinearities.

