\end{table}

The Calvo probabilities $\xi_p$ and $\xi_w$ also correlate negatively: stickier prices compress endogenous shock propagation, keeping the economy closer to steady state. Figure~\ref{fig:param_correlations} ranks all 18 parameters by their correlation with $\delta_j$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/fig_param_correlations.pdf}
\caption{Correlation of each parameter with the nonlinearity measure $\delta = \|g^{\text{FOM}} - g^{\text{ROM1}}\|_2$, sorted by absolute magnitude. Red bars indicate parameters whose increase is associated with more nonlinearity (higher volatility, more persistent shocks); blue bars indicate parameters whose increase \textit{reduces} nonlinearity (Kimball curvature, Calvo probabilities). The Kimball curvature $\varepsilon_p$ shows a negative correlation: flatter demand compresses price dispersion and makes equilibrium dynamics more nearly linear.}
\label{fig:param_correlations}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:nonlinearity_landscape} visualizes the joint structure: on log-log axes, $\delta \propto d$ with a fan pattern---lower Kimball curvature (warm colors) produces more nonlinearity at any given distance.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{figures/fig_nonlinearity_landscape_log.pdf}
\caption{Nonlinearity landscape on log-log axes. Each point is a training sample; horizontal axis is state distance $\|s - \bar{s}\|_2$, vertical axis is the nonlinearity measure $\|g^{\text{FOM}} - g^{\text{ROM1}}\|_2$. Color encodes Kimball demand curvature $\varepsilon_p$ (red = low, yellow = high). The near-linear log-log relationship ($R^2 = 0.96$) confirms that nonlinearity grows proportionally with displacement. The color separation shows that higher Kimball curvature systematically reduces nonlinearity at any given distance.}
\label{fig:nonlinearity_landscape}
\end{figure}


\paragraph{Current-period shocks contribute negligibly.}

I regress $\delta_j$ on current-period shock realizations. The $R^2$ is 0.0003---today's innovation is too small to move the state appreciably at the training shock scale. Nonlinearity accumulates through \textit{persistent} displacement, not single-period shocks. At larger scales (sufficient to trigger the ZLB), the current-period channel may become more important; a supplementary dataset at shock scale 0.4 would test this.


\paragraph{Per-observable nonlinearity.}

I decompose the FOM--ROM1 gap by observable (Table~\ref{tab:per_obs_r2}). Parameter-explained $R^2$ values are uniformly below 12 percent, confirming state-distance dominance across all variables. Output, consumption, and investment growth show the highest parameter sensitivity ($\approx$11--12\%); the interest rate shows the least (6.7\%), consistent with the ZLB's binary nature.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-Observable Nonlinearity Explained by Parameter Groups}
\label{tab:per_obs_r2}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Observable & $R^2$ (structural) & $R^2$ (volatilities) & $R^2$ (persistence) & $R^2$ (all params) \\
\midrule
Output growth ($\Delta y$) & 0.018 & 0.061 & 0.046 & 0.114 \\
Consumption growth ($\Delta c$) & 0.019 & 0.062 & 0.048 & 0.117 \\
Investment growth ($\Delta i$) & 0.016 & 0.058 & 0.046 & 0.111 \\
Hours ($l$) & 0.012 & 0.049 & 0.039 & 0.092 \\
Inflation ($\pi$) & 0.015 & 0.054 & 0.039 & 0.100 \\
Wage growth ($\Delta w$) & 0.015 & 0.056 & 0.042 & 0.104 \\
