\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize Notes: $R^2$ from OLS of per-variable $|\text{FOM} - \text{ROM1}|$ on parameter groups.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Across all variables, shock volatilities carry the largest marginal explanatory power among the three parameter groups (roughly 4--6 percent), followed by persistence parameters (3--5 percent) and structural parameters (1--2 percent). The dominance of volatilities over structural parameters in explaining nonlinearity may seem surprising, given that structural parameters govern the functional form of the equilibrium conditions. The resolution is indirect: higher volatility does not change the curvature of the model equations, but it pushes the economy more frequently into regions where that curvature binds---the state-distance channel again, operating through the ergodic distribution of states.


\paragraph{Surrogate accuracy across the nonlinearity gradient.}

The decomposition has a direct implication for surrogate design: accuracy must hold up far from steady state, where ROM1 fails and the nonlinear correction is largest. Table~\ref{tab:error_by_distance} already showed this for distance quintiles. To further assess surrogate robustness, I sort the test set by parameter region (distance from prior mean in each parameter dimension) and report RMSE ratios between extreme terciles.

Table~\ref{tab:param_region_rmse} shows that the surrogate's RMSE varies modestly across parameter regions. The largest ratio of high-to-low RMSE is 1.68 (for $\sigma_b$), meaning the surrogate is 68 percent less accurate in the high-volatility region of the risk premium shock. For the Kimball parameter, the pattern reverses: RMSE is 25 percent \textit{lower} in the high-$\varepsilon_p$ region (ratio 0.75), consistent with the finding that higher Kimball curvature makes the model more linear and therefore easier to approximate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Surrogate RMSE by Parameter Region (Selected Parameters)}
\label{tab:param_region_rmse}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Parameter & RMSE (low) & RMSE (mid) & RMSE (high) & Ratio (high/low) \\
\midrule
$\rho_g$ & 1.198 & 1.247 & 1.721 & 1.44 \\
$\sigma_b$ & 1.093 & 1.189 & 1.831 & 1.68 \\
$\sigma_w$ & 1.194 & 1.273 & 1.704 & 1.43 \\
$\varepsilon_p$ & 1.538 & 1.491 & 1.150 & 0.75 \\
$\xi_p$ & 1.415 & 1.539 & 1.243 & 0.88 \\
$\iota_p$ & 1.512 & 1.457 & 1.227 & 0.81 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize Notes: Low/mid/high are parameter terciles. RMSE is over all state variables.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The overall pattern confirms that the surrogate provides reliable accuracy across the full parameter support, with graceful degradation in the regions that generate the most nonlinearity. The largest accuracy loss occurs exactly where ROM1 accuracy loss is catastrophic, so even the surrogate's worst-case region represents a massive improvement over the linear baseline.


\paragraph{Discussion.}
Four lessons emerge. First, the nonlinearity that matters for estimation in this model is in the investment channel---not the pricing block. Second, accuracy must hold up far from steady state, where ROM1 fails most. Third, parameter interactions matter: the surrogate's parameter-conditioning architecture learns these without pre-specifying them. Fourth, the nonlinear correction depends on accumulated state displacement, not current-period shocks---which justifies the state-distance gate design (Section~\ref{subsec:regime_switching}).

\paragraph{Testable predictions for posterior shifts.}
The investment-dominance finding predicts that (i) $\sigma_b$ should increase under nonlinear estimation (endogenous amplification in the investment block allows the same fit with larger risk-premium shocks); (ii) $\rho_w$ and $\rho_\pi$ should decrease (nonlinear propagation replaces exogenous persistence); and (iii) $\varepsilon_p$ should increase (the investment channel reduces the pricing block's role). Suggestive evidence from the preliminary chains in Section~\ref{subsec:results_18param_switching} is directionally consistent with these predictions: $\sigma_b$ doubles, $\rho_w$ falls by 80 percent, and $\varepsilon_p$ nearly triples, though the chains have not converged and surrogate approximation error may contribute.


\subsection{State-Dependent Impulse Responses: SEP FOM vs.\ ROM1}
\label{subsec:irf_comparison}

I compute impulse response functions under ROM1 and SEP FOM for three shocks---investment-specific ($\varepsilon^{qs}$), risk premium ($\varepsilon^b$), and price markup ($\varepsilon^{\pi}$)---at magnitudes $1\sigma$ through $5\sigma$ with both signs. Under ROM1, responses scale linearly and are symmetric; under SEP FOM, neither property holds.

\paragraph{ROM1 vs.\ SEP FOM comparison.}

Figure~\ref{fig:sep_vs_rom1_eqs} shows the investment-specific shock results. At $1\sigma$ the responses coincide; by $5\sigma$ the divergence is substantial---positive shocks are amplified (quadratic $S''$ compounds with the Euler wedge) and negative shocks are asymmetrically attenuated (convex $a(z)$ dampens downward adjustment).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_sep_vs_rom1_eqs_investment.pdf}
\caption{Investment growth impulse responses: ROM1 (dashed) vs.\ SEP FOM (solid) for the investment-specific shock at magnitudes of $1\sigma$ through $5\sigma$. Positive shocks shown in red, negative shocks in dark red (dash-dot). At small shocks the two coincide; at large shocks the SEP FOM shows amplification (positive) and asymmetric attenuation (negative) that the linear model cannot represent. Horizon: 40 quarters from steady state.}
\label{fig:sep_vs_rom1_eqs}
\end{figure}

The risk premium shock (Figure~\ref{fig:sep_vs_rom1_eb}) propagates through asset prices to Tobin's $q$, again concentrating nonlinear amplification in investment growth. The price markup shock (Figure~\ref{fig:sep_vs_rom1_epinf}) shows minimal ROM1--SEP divergence even at $5\sigma$, confirming the pricing block's negligible contribution.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_sep_vs_rom1_eb_investment.pdf}
\caption{Investment growth impulse responses: ROM1 vs.\ SEP FOM for the risk premium shock. Format as in Figure~\ref{fig:sep_vs_rom1_eqs}. The nonlinear amplification propagates through the Euler equation to Tobin's $q$ and investment.}
\label{fig:sep_vs_rom1_eb}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_sep_vs_rom1_epinf_investment.pdf}
\caption{Investment growth impulse responses: ROM1 vs.\ SEP FOM for the price markup shock. Format as in Figure~\ref{fig:sep_vs_rom1_eqs}. The ROM1--SEP divergence is minimal even at $5\sigma$, confirming that the pricing block is not a major source of nonlinearity.}
\label{fig:sep_vs_rom1_epinf}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Nonlinear correction surface.}

I construct the correction surface $C(k, h) = \text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}_{h}(k\sigma) - \text{IRF}^{\text{ROM1}}_{h}(k\sigma)$ (Figure~\ref{fig:correction_surface_eqs}). The correction peaks in the first 5--10 quarters and grows nonlinearly with shock size---the $5\sigma$ correction is not five times the $1\sigma$ correction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/fig_nonlinear_correction_surface_eqs.pdf}
\caption{Nonlinear correction surface for the investment-specific shock: $C(k,h) = \text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}(k\sigma, h) - \text{IRF}^{\text{ROM1}}(k\sigma, h)$. Red indicates positive correction (SEP amplifies relative to ROM1); blue indicates negative correction. The correction peaks in the first 5--10 quarters and grows nonlinearly with shock magnitude.}
\label{fig:correction_surface_eqs}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{IRF asymmetry.}

I measure IRF asymmetry as $A(k,h) = |\text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}_{h}(+k\sigma)| - |\text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}_{h}(-k\sigma)|$ (Figure~\ref{fig:asymmetry_eqs}). The asymmetry grows with shock size, reflecting the convex utilization cost: positive shocks push into the steep region of $a(z)$, negative shocks into the flat region.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/fig_irf_asymmetry_eqs.pdf}
\caption{IRF asymmetry surface for the investment-specific shock: $A(k,h) = |\text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}(+k\sigma, h)| - |\text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}(-k\sigma, h)|$. Red indicates the positive shock has a larger absolute effect; blue indicates the negative shock dominates. The asymmetry grows with shock magnitude and concentrates at short horizons.}
\label{fig:asymmetry_eqs}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Multi-variable response.}

Figure~\ref{fig:all_vars_eqs} shows the full seven-observable response to a $4\sigma$ investment-specific shock. Nonlinear amplification concentrates in investment growth, spills to output and hours, but leaves inflation and the interest rate nearly unchanged between ROM1 and SEP FOM.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_sep_all_vars_eqs_4sigma.pdf}
\caption{All-observable impulse responses to a $4\sigma$ investment-specific shock. ROM1 (blue dashed), SEP FOM positive (red solid), SEP FOM negative (dark red dash-dot). The nonlinear correction concentrates in investment growth, with spillovers to output and hours. Inflation and the interest rate are nearly unchanged between ROM1 and SEP FOM.}
\label{fig:all_vars_eqs}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Amplification ratio.}

Figure~\ref{fig:amplification_ratio} plots the amplification ratio (peak $|\text{IRF}^{\text{SEP}}|$ / peak $|\text{IRF}^{\text{ROM1}}|$) by shock type and magnitude. Investment-specific and risk premium shocks show increasing amplification; the price markup ratio stays near one---a clean internal control consistent with the investment channel driving the nonlinear amplification.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/fig_nonlinear_amplification_ratio.pdf}
\caption{Nonlinear amplification ratio for investment growth by shock type and magnitude. A ratio above one indicates nonlinear amplification. The dashed gray line marks the linear benchmark.}
\label{fig:amplification_ratio}
\end{figure}


\subsection{ROM1 Forecast Errors in Real Data}
\label{subsec:rom1_forecast_errors}

I run the ROM1 inversion filter on US quarterly data (1959Q1--2004Q4) to test whether the same pattern appears in real data. Figure~\ref{fig:rom1_forecast_errors} plots one-step-ahead forecast errors. Investment growth errors dominate: RMSE of 2.69 versus 1.27 (output) and 0.92 (inflation), accounting for 53 percent of total forecast error variance. During crisis quarters (13 of 184, defined as $|\text{innovation}_{\Delta i}| > 2\sigma$), investment RMSE jumps to 10.4 ($6.3\times$ normal) while inflation errors actually \emph{fall} ($0.83\times$). The recovered investment-specific shock is $4.4\times$ larger during crises; the price markup shock is unchanged ($0.94\times$). The pattern is asymmetric---large negative investment surprises are not offset by equally large positive ones---consistent with convex $a(z)$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_rom1_forecast_errors.pdf}
\caption{ROM1 one-step-ahead forecast errors for US data. Top panel: investment growth; middle: output growth; bottom: inflation. Shaded areas mark NBER recessions. Dashed red lines mark $\pm 2\sigma$ bands. Investment growth errors are the largest and spike most dramatically during recessions, consistent with the equation-block decomposition finding that the investment/capital block accounts for 69 percent of the FOM--ROM1 nonlinearity gap.}
\label{fig:rom1_forecast_errors}
\end{figure}

The connection to the decomposition is direct: 68.8 percent (training) versus 53.2 percent (real data) of the gap concentrates in investment growth. The rolling 20-quarter RMSE peaks at $2.67\times$ average during the Great Recession for investment versus $1.60\times$ for inflation (Figure~\ref{fig:rolling_rmse}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_rolling_rmse.pdf}
\caption{Rolling 20-quarter RMSE of ROM1 one-step-ahead forecast errors by observable. Investment growth (red) dominates forecast error intensity and shows the sharpest spikes during recessions. Inflation (green) remains comparatively stable. The financial crisis of 2007--2009 produces the peak rolling RMSE for investment growth, consistent with the investment channel generating the largest ROM1 approximation errors when the economy moves farthest from steady state.}
\label{fig:rolling_rmse}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:crisis_vs_normal} summarizes the full crisis--normal comparison. Investment, consumption, and output errors are 5--6$\times$ larger during crisis; inflation errors shrink. This pattern is consistent with nonlinear investment dynamics generating the largest ROM1 approximation errors during stress episodes.

