\end{table}

\paragraph{Negative control: a model without investment.}

As a negative control, the Gali (2015) model---no capital, investment, or Tobin's $q$---shows an average ROM1--ROM2 gap of 10.5 percent, roughly one-seventh of the HLT model's 75 percent (Figure~\ref{fig:gali_vs_hlt_nonlinearity}). The monetary policy shock gap is just 2.0 percent.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/fig_gali_vs_hlt_nonlinearity.pdf}
\caption{Average ROM1--ROM2 impulse response gap. Gali model: simple NK without capital/investment (23 variables, 3 shocks). HLT model: full Smets--Wouters with investment channel (66 variables, 7 shocks). Gaps are computed across all shocks and shock sizes ($1$--$5\sigma$). The HLT model's nonlinearity gap is 7--8 times larger than the Gali model's, concentrated in the investment and capital accumulation channel.}
\label{fig:gali_vs_hlt_nonlinearity}
\end{figure}

The gap percentages are constant across shock sizes---a mechanical consequence of the perturbation architecture (second-order correction is exactly quadratic in state deviation)---confirming genuine curvature effects rather than numerical artifacts.

\paragraph{Why second-order perturbation is not a sufficient benchmark.}
ROM2 captures only the quadratic Taylor term ($\mathcal{O}(\|\hat{s}\|^3)$ error). The exponential $a(z)$ has nonzero coefficients at every order, and the multiplicative $S \times q^s \times p^k$ interaction generates cubic cross-terms that ROM2 misses. The correct benchmark is SEP FOM; the ROM1--ROM2 comparison serves only as a qualitative cross-model diagnostic.


