These serve as the linear benchmark.

\paragraph{Regime-switching surrogate estimation.}
\label{subsec:results_18param_switching}

I estimate the same 18 parameters using the regime-switching surrogate. The gate identifies $\approx$10 percent of periods as nonlinear---concentrating around the Volcker disinflation, the 1990s recession, and the dot-com bust. I draw 50,000 samples via MH; runtime is 50 minutes ($\approx$5$\times$ slower than Kalman). The log-likelihood at posterior mean is $-1{,}108$ versus $-1{,}040$ for Kalman; the gap reflects shock-conditioning differences and surrogate approximation error.

Table~\ref{tab:18param_switching_posterior} compares posteriors. Three patterns stand out. First, $\sigma_b$ doubles from 0.12 to 0.28 (90\% CI: [0.11, 1.06])---the nonlinear model assigns a larger role to risk premium shocks, consistent with investment-block amplification. Second, persistence parameters fall: $\rho_w$ drops from 0.37 to 0.08 and $\rho_{ms}$ from 0.18 to 0.07, suggesting the nonlinear model accounts for low-frequency variation through propagation rather than exogenous persistence. Third, $\varepsilon_p$ nearly triples from 39 to 104, implying the nonlinear model achieves inflation persistence through real rigidities rather than backward-looking indexation.

These shifts connect directly to the decomposition: the investment block accounts for 69 percent of the FOM--ROM1 gap, and the parameters that shift most ($\sigma_b$, $\rho_w$, $\varepsilon_p$) are those that interact most strongly with the investment channel. The linear model may compensate for missing amplification by compressing risk premium volatility and inflating markup persistence. Two caveats: MCMC chains have not fully converged (Section~\ref{subsec:known_limitations}), and surrogate error is non-negligible. A definitive decomposition requires the full-order SEP posterior.

