\paragraph{Convergence diagnostics.} Both 50,000-draw chains are insufficient for the 18-parameter space. $\hat{R}$ exceeds 1.1 for 11 of 18 RS parameters (max 2.67 for $\rho_{ms}$) and 12 of 18 Kalman parameters (max 3.62 for $\rho_b$), with median ESS of 65 (RS) and 46 (Kalman). The well-identified parameters---shock volatilities and Kimball curvature---converge ($\hat{R} < 1.05$, ESS $> 200$), but persistence parameters require longer chains. Results should be treated as preliminary.

\paragraph{Scope limitations.} The training data use shock scale $\sigma = 0.1$, at which the ZLB never binds (requiring $\approx 12\sigma$). All captured nonlinearity comes from investment adjustment costs and capital utilization, not the constraint. The sample ends in 2004Q4, excluding the 2008 crisis and COVID-19. A supplementary dataset at shock scale 0.4 with ZLB binding and sample extension through the crisis episodes would provide the most informative test of the method.

\subsection{Runtime}
\label{subsec:results_hlt_runtime}

Table~\ref{tab:benchmarks} compares computational costs across the estimation methods. The surrogate-based regime-switching estimator is roughly 20 times slower per evaluation than the Kalman filter but orders of magnitude faster than a bootstrap particle filter with SEP transitions. The particle filter is embarrassingly parallel across particles; costs scale inversely with core count.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Computational Benchmark Comparison}
\label{tab:benchmarks}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & Per Eval (s) & 50{,}000 MH Draws & Status \\
\midrule
Kalman filter (ROM1) & 0.003 & 2.5 min & Complete \\
Regime-switching surrogate & 0.06 & 50 min & Complete \\
Bootstrap PF + ROM1 ($N_p\!=\!1{,}000$) & $\approx 2$ & $\approx 28$ hours & Projected \\
Bootstrap PF + SEP ($N_p\!=\!100$, 4 cores) & $\approx 3{,}700$ & $\approx 5.8$ years & Projected \\
Bootstrap PF + SEP ($N_p\!=\!100$, 100 cores) & $\approx 150$ & $\approx 87$ days & Projected \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\smallskip\footnotesize
Notes: Kalman and RS surrogate rows are actual wall-clock timing on Apple M4 (4 performance cores). PF rows are projected. PF + ROM1: each particle requires a matrix multiply per period ($\approx$0.01 ms); resampling adds overhead. PF + SEP: each particle requires a full SEP solve (0.8 s) per period; parallelized with $\lceil N_p / N_{\text{cores}} \rceil$ batches per period. On a 100-core cluster, the PF + SEP benchmark becomes feasible as a one-time validation exercise. \citet{gust2012effects} report comparable runtimes for particle-filter estimation of a medium-scale model with regime-specific approximations. Beyond runtime, the particle filter faces weight degeneracy during crisis periods, where effective sample size collapses to near unity \citep{herbst2019tempered}---the filter-free formulation avoids this entirely.
\end{table}

\paragraph{Break-even analysis.}
The offline cost is approximately 12 hours (10 hours dataset generation, 2 hours training). A direct SEP evaluation at $T=184$ costs $\approx$147 seconds per draw versus 0.06 seconds for the surrogate, so the break-even point is $12 \times 3600 / 147 \approx 294$ draws. A 50,000-draw run achieves $\approx$2,450$\times$ speedup. The same surrogate serves multiple estimation runs since $g_\theta(s, \varepsilon)$ does not depend on observed data.

Table~\ref{tab:computational_cost} breaks down the runtime by step for the 3-parameter validation workflow. The offline stage---dataset generation and surrogate training---dominates total time, while the online switching estimation runs in under two minutes in three of four runs. The main runtime variability comes from the switching-estimation step, where one run takes 925 seconds versus 100 seconds for the others.

