\subsection{Extended-Sample Estimation Through 2025}
\label{subsec:extended_sample}

The baseline sample (1959Q1--2004Q4) excludes the episodes where nonlinear dynamics should matter most. Extending to 2025Q1 (311 quarters) and re-estimating under the Kalman filter reveals dramatic shifts: $\varepsilon_p$ rises from 39 to 100 ($+9.3\sigma$), $\sigma_a$ from 0.50 to 0.71 ($+8.6\sigma$), and $\sigma_w$ from 0.20 to 0.39 ($+5.9\sigma$). These are driven by COVID-19: the ROM1 inversion filter recovers wage markup shocks exceeding $38\sigma$ during 2020Q2---events that would occur once every $10^{300}$ quarters under the linear model. The linear transition function may reflect its inability to capture state-dependent propagation through implausibly large shock magnitudes.

The parameters that shift most are those connected to the investment and labor channels, not pricing or monetary policy. The monetary shock parameters $\sigma_{ms}$ and $\rho_{ms}$ barely move despite a decade of near-zero rates, consistent with the decomposition finding that the monetary block contributes less than 0.1 percent of the nonlinearity gap. I treat these results as motivation rather than a central finding: the surrogate has not been retrained for the extended sample's state space, and a definitive exercise requires retraining with extended-trajectory data.


\section{Robustness and Failure Modes}
\label{sec:robustness}

I test whether the main findings survive changes in design choices, initial conditions, and stress scenarios.

\subsection{Initial Condition Robustness}

The influence of the initial state $s_0$ decays geometrically at rate $\max_i |\lambda_i(A)|$. For the SW07 model, $\max_i |\lambda_i| \approx 0.987$, giving a half-life of $\approx$53 quarters. In the 184-quarter sample, the initial condition is negligible after the first five years. In the 3-parameter validation, the NUTS sampler treats $s_0$ as free; its posterior converges to values consistent with steady state (marginal SD $\approx 0.01$). For short samples or near-unit-root parameterizations ($\rho > 0.99$), sensitivity could increase; a formal comparison under displaced and crisis priors for $s_0$ is planned.

\subsection{Stress and Sensitivity Checks}

I test surrogate accuracy in parameter tails, SEP behavior under stress, seed dependence, and prior sensitivity. Table~\ref{tab:surrogate_regional_accuracy} reports RRMSE by parameter quartile:

