Accuracy declines mildly in prior tails but remains within the acceptance threshold. The real rate is the hardest output to approximate, yet posterior summaries remain stable when I double network size.

% \paragraph{SEP Floor-Hits and Crisis Dynamics.}

In the 3-parameter Gal\'i model with baseline shock volatilities, the ZLB binds in 4.2 percent of SEP tree nodes. Under a stress volatility of $\sigma_r^{\text{stress}} = 3 \times \sigma_r^{\text{baseline}}$, binding frequency rises to 18.7 percent, median residual norms stay below $10^{-8}$, and I observe no solver failures. In the 18-parameter Smets--Wouters model, the attenuated shock scale used for reliable SEP convergence (scale factor 0.1) keeps the economy well away from the ZLB. Generating binding ZLB episodes in this model requires shock scales of approximately 0.4, which is feasible but increases SEP solver failures; this extension is left for future work.

\paragraph{Shock-scale sensitivity of the decomposition.}
The investment-dominance finding could in principle be an artifact of the small shock scale ($\sigma = 0.1$) at which the ZLB never binds. Two facts argue against this. First, the IRF comparison uses shocks up to $5\sigma$ and investment dominance \textit{increases} with shock magnitude while the price markup ratio stays flat. Second, the Gali negative control (no investment block) shows a small ROM1--ROM2 gap even at $20\sigma$, confirming that pricing and monetary policy are inherently more linear. Whether ZLB binding at $\sigma = 0.4$ would raise the monetary policy block's contribution from 0.1 percent to a meaningful share remains an open question.


