\subsection{Known Limitations}
\label{subsec:known_limitations}

I am explicit about the method's current limitations, grouped by severity.

\paragraph{Convergence of 18-parameter chains.} Neither chain has converged for all 18 parameters ($\hat{R} > 1.1$ for 11 of 18 RS parameters, median ESS = 65). Shock volatilities and Kimball curvature are reliable; persistence parameters are not. The direction of posterior shifts is consistent across random restarts, but magnitudes are preliminary. Extension to 500,000 draws ($\approx$8 hours) is feasible and in progress.

\paragraph{ZLB non-binding at training scale.} The 18-parameter surrogate is trained on data where the ZLB never binds. All reported nonlinearity comes from the investment adjustment cost and capital utilization cost channels. Whether a ZLB-binding training dataset would produce additional posterior shifts---particularly for the monetary policy parameters ($\rho_{ms}$, $\sigma_{ms}$)---is an open question that requires generating training data at larger shock scales.

\paragraph{Short-sample and near-unit-root fragility.} At $T=50$, interval coverage falls to 82 percent against a 90 percent target. Near-unit-root persistence ($\rho > 0.98$) requires longer SEP horizons ($T_{\text{sim}} > 600$), and models with many jointly binding constraints suffer from tree-growth acceleration.

\paragraph{Surrogate approximation error.} The 442-nat log-likelihood bias bound (equation~\eqref{eq:ll_bias_bound}) exceeds the 68-point Kalman--RS gap, so some posterior shifts could reflect surrogate error rather than genuine nonlinearity. ROM1-anchored training reduces this bound, but a definitive decomposition requires comparison against the full-order SEP posterior.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper develops a multi-fidelity estimator for nonlinear DSGE models with occasionally binding constraints. A neural network surrogate learns the residual between stochastic extended path and first-order perturbation solutions; a state-distance gate activates the surrogate only where the linear approximation fails; and filter-free HMC samples the posterior in minutes rather than days. The 3-parameter validation recovers shock parameters within 5 percent of truth with $\hat{R} < 1.01$ and ESS above 3,800.

The nonlinearity decomposition on an 18-parameter Smets--Wouters model delivers a sharp result: the investment and capital block accounts for 69 percent of the full-order vs.\ first-order gap, while the pricing block contributes 0.2 percent. State distance from steady state explains 96 percent of the residual variance, and current-period shocks contribute less than 0.3 percent---validating a gate design that triggers on state displacement rather than shock magnitudes. IRF comparisons confirm investment-shock amplification ratios that grow with shock size while price markup shocks stay near the linear benchmark.

The 18-parameter estimation on US data (1959Q1--2004Q4) shows that the nonlinear model amplifies the risk premium channel, compresses wage markup persistence, and raises Kimball curvature---consistent with linear estimation misattributing nonlinear propagation to exogenous persistence. The decomposition and IRF results are deterministic measurements from the SEP solver and do not depend on MCMC convergence. The 18-parameter posterior shifts are directionally robust but preliminary pending chain extension.

Extending the sample through 2025Q1 reinforces the case: the ROM1 filter requires $38\sigma$ wage markup shocks during COVID-19, and the parameters that shift most under extension are exactly those connected to the investment and labor channels---not pricing or monetary policy.

Several extensions remain. The gate is heuristic and would benefit from oracle-optimal design that minimizes posterior distortion per unit cost. The surrogate carries no epistemic uncertainty; adding ensemble or conformal bands would enable automatic fallback to direct SEP when confidence is low. A particle MCMC comparison with exact SEP evaluations (feasible at $\approx$87 cluster-days) would provide the gold-standard benchmark. Longer chains, ZLB-binding training data, and a post-2007 estimation sample are all computationally feasible and would sharpen the current results.

The contribution is not that neural networks approximate DSGEs---it is that the approximation gap between fidelity levels can be measured, the switching rule targets precisely where linear methods fail, and the resulting posterior shifts are economically interpretable.


