\end{table}


\section{HMC Implementation}
\label{app:hmc}

This appendix provides implementation details for filter-free Hamiltonian Monte Carlo.


\subsection{Turing.jl Model Specification}

The augmented posterior is implemented in Turing.jl as follows (pseudocode):

\begin{verbatim}
@model function FilterFreeHMC(y_obs, surrogate, T)
    # Priors on structural parameters
    xi_p ~ Beta(7.5, 2.5)
    phi_pi ~ Gamma(3.0, 0.5)
    phi_y ~ Gamma(2.0, 0.0625)
    sigma_a ~ Gamma(2.0, 0.005)
    sigma_mu ~ Gamma(2.0, 0.0075)
    sigma_r ~ Gamma(2.0, 0.00125)

    theta = [xi_p, phi_pi, phi_y, sigma_a, sigma_mu, sigma_r]

    # Prior on initial state (centered at steady state)
    s_0 ~ MvNormal(steady_state(theta), 0.01*I)

    # Prior on latent shocks
    epsilon ~ filldist(Normal(0, 1), T, n_eps)

    # Likelihood via surrogate
    s_prev = s_0
    for t in 1:T
        s_t = surrogate(s_prev, epsilon[t,:], theta)
        y_pred = observation_map(s_t)
        y_obs[t,:] ~ MvNormal(y_pred, Sigma_eta)
        s_prev = s_t
    end
end
\end{verbatim}


\subsection{Gradient Computation Through the Surrogate}

The key computational advantage of HMC over random-walk Metropolis--Hastings is its use of gradient information. The augmented log-posterior is
\[
\log \tilde{p}(\theta, s_0, \varepsilon_{1:T}) = \log p(\theta) + \log p(s_0 \mid \theta) + \sum_t \log p(\varepsilon_t) + \sum_t \log p(y_t^{\text{obs}} \mid s_t),
\]
where $s_t = \hat{g}(s_{t-1}, \varepsilon_t, \theta; w^*)$ is the surrogate-predicted state. The gradient with respect to $\varepsilon_t$ requires backpropagation through the sequential forward simulation: a perturbation to $\varepsilon_t$ changes $s_t$, which changes $s_{t+1}$ through the next surrogate evaluation, and so on to $s_T$. This chain-rule computation is precisely the reverse-mode automatic differentiation that neural networks are designed for. Julia's Zygote.jl and ReverseDiff.jl libraries compute these gradients automatically from the Turing.jl model specification.

The gradient with respect to $\theta$ is more expensive: $\theta$ enters every surrogate evaluation (because the surrogate is parameter-conditional), so the chain rule passes through all $T$ surrogate calls. The cost of a single gradient evaluation is therefore $\mathcal{O}(T \cdot C_{\text{surr}})$, where $C_{\text{surr}}$ is the cost of one surrogate forward-backward pass. For the [256, 128] architecture (43,158 weights in the 3-parameter case; 49,562 in the 18-parameter case, due to the larger input dimension), a single forward-backward pass takes approximately 0.5 ms, so the gradient of the full $T=200$ log-posterior takes approximately $200 \times 0.5 = 100$ ms. This is fast enough for NUTS, which typically evaluates 50--100 gradient calls per HMC draw (median tree depth 6--7, with each leapfrog step requiring one gradient evaluation).

For the 18-parameter Metropolis--Hastings estimation, gradients are not used---the proposal is a random walk. The surrogate's differentiability is available but not exploited, because the inversion filter already reduces the sampling dimension from $n_\theta + n_s + T n_\varepsilon$ to $n_\theta$, making gradient-based proposals less critical. Combining the inversion filter with gradient-based proposals (e.g., MALA or HMC over the 18-dimensional $\theta$ space with inversion-filter shock recovery) is a natural next step that would improve mixing.

\subsection{NUTS Tuning}

The No-U-Turn Sampler automatically tunes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Step size $\eps$}: Adaptive dual averaging targets acceptance rate $\delta=0.80$. Initial $\eps=0.01$, adjusted during warmup.

\item \textbf{Mass matrix $M$}: Estimated from warmup samples. I use diagonal adaptation:
\begin{equation}
M = \text{diag}(\text{Var}[\theta], \text{Var}[s_0], \text{Var}[\eps_{1:T}]),
\end{equation}
where variances are computed empirically.

\item \textbf{Tree depth}: Automatically determined by the no-U-turn criterion (median depth $\approx 6$ for this problem).
\end{enumerate}


\subsection{Diagnostic Computation}

\paragraph{Gelman--Rubin $\hat{R}$}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{R} = \sqrt{\frac{\hat{V}}{W}}, \quad \hat{V} = \frac{n-1}{n} W + \frac{1}{n} B,
\end{equation}
where $W$ is within-chain variance and $B$ is between-chain variance.

\paragraph{Effective sample size (ESS)}:
\begin{equation}
\text{ESS} = \frac{N}{1 + 2\sum_{k=1}^\infty \hat{\rho}_k},
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\rho}_k$ is autocorrelation at lag $k$, truncated when $\hat{\rho}_k < 0.05$.

\paragraph{Energy diagnostics}: NUTS-specific. I compute the energy transition distribution and flag chains if the fraction of bad transitions $> 0.1$.


\section{Computational Details}
\label{app:computational}

\subsection{Hardware and Software}

All experiments run on:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{CPU}: Apple M4, 4 performance cores + 6 efficiency cores
\item \textbf{RAM}: 16 GB unified memory
\item \textbf{OS}: macOS Sequoia 15
\end{itemize}

Software versions:
\begin{itemize}
\item Julia 1.12.4
\item Turing.jl 0.35.3
\item Flux.jl 0.16.3 (neural network training)
\item MacroModelling.jl (DSGE model specification and SEP solver)
\item LinearAlgebra, Statistics, Serialization (Julia standard library)
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Reproducibility}

All code and data are available at:
\begin{center}
\texttt{https://github.com/matyasfarkas/SurrogateNN\_Estimation.jl}
\end{center}

To reproduce the 3-parameter validation:
\begin{verbatim}
julia> using SurrogateDSGE
julia> include("scripts/run_3param_validation.jl")
\end{verbatim}

Expected strict-smoke runtime: about 17--31 minutes on the reference machine, with most runs near 17--18 minutes (Table~\ref{tab:computational_cost}).


